<Project>

    <!-- Safety net: any exe project in this repo gets System.Memory copied next to the exe.

         Why: the Tekla packages are referenced with ExcludeAssets="runtime", which also strips the
         transitive System.Memory (and System.Buffers, System.Numerics.Vectors,
         System.Runtime.CompilerServices.Unsafe) from a standalone exe's output. The exe then dies
         on first Tekla connect with
         "Could not load file or assembly 'System.Memory, Version=4.0.1.2'".
         TeklaDump.Cli carries an explicit System.Memory reference, but that line is easy to drop
         when a csproj is edited or a new exe project is hand-added — this re-injects it so the
         build stays correct regardless.

         Why here and not Directory.Build.props: props are imported BEFORE the project body, where
         OutputType is not yet set; targets are imported AFTER, so the condition below works.

         The duplicate guard keeps this from doubling the reference (NU1504) in projects that
         already declare it. TeklaDump.dll and the macro bundle are untouched — they run INSIDE
         Tekla's process, which provides System.Memory itself, and a stray copy next to a plugin is
         a binding hazard rather than a fix. -->
    <ItemGroup Condition="('$(OutputType)' == 'Exe' or '$(OutputType)' == 'WinExe') and '$(SkipSystemMemoryInjection)' != 'true' and '@(PackageReference->AnyHaveMetadataValue(`Identity`, `System.Memory`))' != 'true'">
        <PackageReference Include="System.Memory" Version="4.6.3" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!-- 4.6.3, not the 4.5.5 that older write-ups on standalone Tekla exes name: the Tekla
         packages themselves depend on System.Memory >= 4.6.3 on every supported version, 2021
         floor included, so 4.5.5 is a package downgrade that NuGet fails the build over. The
         assembly version moves from 4.0.1.2 to 4.0.2.0 with it, which is why TeklaDump.Cli turns
         on AutoGenerateBindingRedirects — see the comment there. -->

    <!-- SkipSystemMemoryInjection is for the two exes that reference the Tekla packages WITH their
         runtime assets (the schema generator and the test project). Those get System.Memory
         through the normal transitive graph at whatever version Tekla asks for, and pinning 4.5.5
         underneath that is a package DOWNGRADE, which NuGet fails the build over. Neither of them
         is a distributed Tekla-connecting exe, so the 4.5.5 pairing does not apply to them. -->

</Project>
